{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790301077,"nonce":"whE/SyY7TAJhG2J9Qz9Zg2dhsYI=","title":"Lua: collatz's LLVM region pays NaN checks on every mixed int/float compare","message":"With the LLVM entry ABI for aggregates (acaa5455) collatz's outlined region lua$main$1$resume0 compiles at tier 1 and the frame transfers into it. On the merged main, that LLVM region runs no faster than the Cranelift region did.\n\nMac arm64, load about 5, crates/zyntax_lua/benchmarks/run.py --runs 3 --only collatz, interleaved:\n- reference 8844ca79 (region on Cranelift): 358 to 370 ms elapsed, 387 to 401 ms wall\n- package branch 1ba93590 alone (region on LLVM, before 72731f12): 186.5 to 187.8 ms elapsed, 218 ms wall\n- main acaa5455 (region on LLVM, with 72731f12): 367.4 to 369.4 ms elapsed, 397 to 401 ms wall\n- LuaJIT: 175 ms elapsed, 180 ms wall\n\nThe pre-opt IR of the region differs only in 72731f12's change: `fcmp one` became `fcmp une`. The Number compare `x \u003e 1` and the float `x % 2 == 0` test each lower to a NaN guard `fcmp une x, x`, and a floor-and-range check before converting. With `one`, `fcmp one x, x` folded to false, so LLVM dropped the guards and unswitched the loop on the tag. That fold was wrong for NaN. With `une` the guards stay, and the tag stays an i64 phi in the loop.\n\nThe fix belongs in the lowering. A float compared with an integer constant that is exact in a double (|k| \u003c= 2^53) is just `fcmp` against (double)k, which is already false for NaN. That needs no NaN guard and no integral check. Dump with ZYNTAX_DUMP_LLVM_IR=1 (the region is zyntax_llvm_ir_*_0002.ll).","files":null}]}